In the realm of fame and fortune there is none;  
My official heart has grown distant from it all.  
I hold only a small salary as a county magistrate;  
The old books on the shelf have been eaten by worms.  
The autumn frost has withered the wildflowers near my door;  
I resign myself to return home.

Translation:
In the world of wealth and power, nothing remains;  
My official sentiments have become estranged.  
I am merely a minor official who earns a meager wage;  
The ancient books on the shelves have been devoured by moths.  
The early morning frost has left the wildflowers by the gate;  
I retire and return home.

This elegy captures the poet's resignation after a period of political disillusionment, expressing his desire for peace and solitude amidst the chaos of worldly ambition. The imagery of worm-eaten books and withered flowers conveys the poet's sense of loss and decay, while the act of returning home symbolizes his retreat from public life. The overall tone is one of melancholy acceptance, reflecting the poet's contemplation of mortality and the transient nature of earthly pursuits.

Bian Weizhi (I)  
Through the lattice window, wind blows through the willow trees;  
The long sky moon casts shadows on the osmanthus tree.  
Coldly carved incense sticks burn like gold beasts;  
The dripping jade pot sounds like a copper dragon's leak.  
Where is the silver flute? Its sound echoes across the clouds;  
It follows the gentle breeze over short posts,  
And just as I hear celestial music from above,  
My body feels it has entered an ethereal realm.

On the road where tender leaves break through the soil,  
Eastward neighbor's eggs have already hatched.  
In the gentle breeze on the hillside, calves moo softly;  
The distant sun sets, and dark trees scatter shadows with dusk.  
Far off mountains stretch out, roads twist and turn;  
A sign for wine is set up, and people gather nearby.  
In the city, peach blossoms and apricot flowers fear storms;  
But spring lies in the fields of wildflowers along the stream.

Translation:
On the road where tender leaves break through the soil,  
Eggs from Eastward Neighbor's nest have already hatched.  
Mooing calves on the hillside, soft as whispers;  
Dusk descends, casting shadows over the dark trees.  
Mountains far away, roads winding here and there;  
Signs for wine are posted, folk gather near.  
In the city, peach blossoms and apricot flowers fear storms;  
Yet spring dwells among the wildflowers by the stream.
